@Column attributes:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id", length = 20)  // column name + VARCHAR(20)
    private String accountId;

    @Column(name = "holder_name", nullable = false, length = 100)
    // NOT NULL + VARCHAR(100). Default: nullable=true, length=255
    private String holderName;

    @Column(nullable = false, precision = 15, scale = 2)
    // DECIMAL(15,2) NOT NULL. Default column name: "balance" (field name)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    @Column(name = "pan_number", unique = true, length = 10)
    // UNIQUE constraint + VARCHAR(10)
    private String panNumber;

    @Column(columnDefinition = "TEXT")
    // Raw DDL: column type is TEXT (overrides Hibernate's default VARCHAR)
    private String address;

    @Column(name = "created_at", updatable = false)
    // updatable=false: this column is set on INSERT only, never updated
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @Column(insertable = false, updatable = false)
    // insertable=false + updatable=false: Hibernate NEVER writes this column
    // Used for: DB-generated columns (triggers, computed columns)
    private LocalDateTime lastModified;
}
```

Without @Column — defaults:

```java
@Entity
public class Trade {
    @Id
    private String tradeId;     // column: trade_id (Spring naming strategy: camelCase → snake_case)
    private String symbol;       // column: symbol (same name)
    private BigDecimal amount;   // column: amount, type: DECIMAL (Hibernate default for BigDecimal)
    // All nullable, no length constraint, no uniqueness
    // For production: ALWAYS use @Column with explicit constraints!
}
```

JPA @Transient vs Java transient:

```java
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private BigDecimal savingsBalance;
    private BigDecimal currentBalance;

    // ═══ JPA @Transient: excluded from DB mapping ═══
    @Transient  // jakarta.persistence.Transient
    private BigDecimal totalBalance;
    // NO column created! Hibernate ignores this field completely.
    // Calculated in code: totalBalance = savingsBalance + currentBalance
    // Not stored in DB — computed on demand.

    @Transient
    private String displayName;
    // Calculated: displayName = holderName + " (" + accountId + ")"
    // No DB column. Computed for API response.

    // ═══ Java transient: excluded from SERIALIZATION only! ═══
    private transient String cachedReport;
    // transient keyword: ObjectOutputStream SKIPS this field during serialization.
    // BUT: Hibernate STILL maps it to a column! DB has "cached_report" column!
    // This is probably NOT what you want for a cached/computed field!

    // ═══ THE CONFUSION ═══
    // @Transient (JPA): skip DB mapping (no column)
    // transient (Java): skip serialization (column STILL created!)
    // For computed fields: use @Transient (JPA), NOT transient (Java keyword)
}
```

@PostLoad for computed @Transient fields:

```java
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id private String accountId;
    private String firstName;
    private String lastName;
    private BigDecimal savingsBalance;
    private BigDecimal currentBalance;

    @Transient
    private String fullName;

    @Transient
    private BigDecimal totalBalance;

    @PostLoad  // called AFTER entity is loaded from DB
    public void computeTransientFields() {
        this.fullName = firstName + " " + lastName;
        this.totalBalance = savingsBalance.add(currentBalance);
    }
    // fullName and totalBalance computed AFTER loading — available for use
    // but NEVER stored in DB (no columns exist for them).
}
```

